# PulseAudio, BUILT AS A CLIENT LIBRARY AND NOT AS A SOUND SERVER.
#
# READ THIS FIRST OR THE FLAGS BELOW LOOK LIKE VANDALISM. This tree already has
# a sound server: pipewire, built -Dpipewire-alsa=enabled, which also ships
# pipewire-pulse -- its own implementation of the PulseAudio protocol. Two
# servers would compete for the same devices and the same socket, and the one
# that lost would be this one. What is missing is the other half: the CLIENT
# side of that protocol, the libraries an application links to speak to a pulse
# server. pipewire deliberately does not provide those (its own recipe passes
# -Dlibpulse=disabled, which is a different thing again -- a tunnel to a remote
# pulse server).
#
# So this package exists to produce three libraries and nothing else:
#
#   libpulse                 the client API
#   libpulse-simple          the synchronous convenience wrapper
#   libpulse-mainloop-glib   THE ONE THIS WAVE IS FOR. gnome-settings-daemon
#                            hard-requires libpulse-mainloop-glib; its media-keys
#                            and sound plugins drive volume through it. Nothing
#                            else in the tree supplies it.
#
# -Ddaemon=false IS THE LOAD-BEARING FLAG and it does far more than remove a
# binary. meson.build:656 opens `if get_option('daemon')` around the ENTIRE
# daemon-and-module dependency block, so with it false the following are never
# looked up at all: alsa, udev, bluez5, sbc, avahi, openssl, jack, lirc, speex,
# soxr, samplerate, orc, webrtc-aec, fftw, systemd, elogind, tcpwrap, gsettings,
# and -- the two that make the point -- `cc.find_library('ltdl', required: true)`
# and `dependency('tdb')`, both UNCONDITIONAL inside that block. tdb is not
# packaged in this tree at all, so a daemon build is not merely undesirable
# here, it does not configure. (BLFS sidesteps that with -Ddatabase=gdbm; gdbm
# is not packaged either.)
#
# THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS FILE, stated because it is the opposite of the
# pipewire recipe's approach and the difference is deliberate: the flags below
# do NOT enumerate every option. Every option the daemon block owns is
# unreachable, and listing thirty inert flags would suggest they were doing
# something. What IS enumerated is every option consulted in a client-only
# build. The risk that leaves -- someone flipping -Ddaemon=true later and
# silently acquiring twenty auto-resolved dependencies -- is held by
# install.sh's assertion that the daemon is absent, which fails that build
# rather than shipping it.
#
# BLFS 12.4, pinned here rather than in versions.env: versions.env is
# regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by tools/pin-versions.sh, so a desktop
# pin placed there is destroyed by the next `make pin`.
SRC_URL=https://www.freedesktop.org/software/pulseaudio/releases/pulseaudio-17.0.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=053794d6671a3e397d849e478a80b82a63cb9d8ca296bd35b73317bb5ceb87b5
SRC_DIR=pulseaudio-17.0

# THE OPTIONS CONSULTED IN A CLIENT-ONLY BUILD, each stated including the ones
# that equal their default, because five of the six defaults here are wrong for
# this tree.
#
#   -Dglib=enabled       NOT OPTIONAL DESPITE BEING A FEATURE OPTION. It
#                        defaults to 'auto', glib is packaged, so auto would
#                        resolve -- and then this package's entire purpose would
#                        be a property of the builder image rather than of the
#                        recipe. src/pulse/meson.build:118 builds
#                        libpulse-mainloop-glib ONLY inside `if glib_dep.found()`,
#                        and meson.build:888 adds libpulse-mainloop-glib.pc to
#                        the install list under the same condition. An image
#                        without glib would produce a green pulseaudio missing
#                        exactly the library gnome-settings-daemon needs, and
#                        nothing in this build would say so.
#
#   -Ddbus=enabled       the session bus, which is how a client discovers a
#                        server address it was not told about. Also 'auto' with
#                        dbus packaged, so this states the outcome rather than
#                        inheriting it.
#
#   -Dgtk=disabled       gtk_dep is `dependency('gtk+-3.0', required:
#                        get_option('gtk'))` inside the CLIENT block
#                        (meson.build:648) -- and gtk3 IS packaged in this tree.
#                        Auto would therefore resolve it and quietly add a GTK 3
#                        edge to a package whose description names no toolkit.
#                        The README is explicit that the gtk3 island "must not
#                        become somewhere GTK 3 packages accumulate"; this is
#                        what that costs in practice.
#
#   -Dx11=disabled       `dependency('x11-xcb', required: get_option('x11'))`,
#                        auto, and libX11 is packaged so auto resolves. It
#                        builds pax11publish, which publishes the pulse server
#                        address into X root window properties -- an X11-session
#                        mechanism with no meaning in a Wayland session, and one
#                        that would pull x11-xcb, xcb, ice, sm and xtst in
#                        behind it (meson.build:750-757, all four `required:
#                        true` once x11 is found).
#
#   -Dsystemd=disabled   FINDING 29, VERBATIM AND OUTSIDE THE DAEMON BLOCK.
#                        meson.build:617 is `dependency('libsystemd', required:
#                        get_option('systemd'))` and elogind ships a
#                        libsystemd.pc symlink precisely so GNOME components
#                        resolve it -- so 'auto' on a system with no systemd
#                        FINDS ONE, and sets HAVE_SYSTEMD_DAEMON,
#                        HAVE_SYSTEMD_LOGIN and HAVE_SYSTEMD_JOURNAL together.
#                        Three journal/login code paths compiled against
#                        elogind's compatibility surface, in the client library,
#                        because of a symlink. Pinned so the outcome is stated
#                        rather than inherited from whichever provider matched
#                        first.
#
#   -Doss-output=disabled
#                        `cc.has_header('sys/soundcard.h', required:
#                        get_option('oss-output'))`, auto -- a HEADER probe, so
#                        it resolves against the kernel headers rather than
#                        against a package, which makes it likelier to succeed
#                        than it looks. It builds padsp, an LD_PRELOAD shim that
#                        makes OSS programs talk to pulse. OSS predates ALSA by
#                        two decades and nothing in this tree emits it. Turning
#                        it off also removes the orphan half of a pair:
#                        shell-completion/bash/meson.build installs a `padsp`
#                        completion symlink for the client unconditionally, so
#                        with the shim built the completion is real, and without
#                        the flag stated it would be a completion for a binary
#                        that may or may not exist depending on a header.
#
#   -Ddoxygen=false      DEFAULTS TRUE, and meson.build:844 then does
#                        `subdir('doxygen')`. Not soft: this is BLFS's one
#                        non-obvious flag on this package and it is on their
#                        command line for the same reason it is on this one.
#
#   -Dtests=false        DEFAULTS TRUE, and meson.build:840 is
#                        `dependency('check', version: '>= 0.9.10', required:
#                        get_option('tests'))` -- a BOOLEAN passed as `required`,
#                        so the default makes the check unit-test framework a
#                        HARD requirement. It is not packaged. An unpinned
#                        pulseaudio does not configure in this tree.
#
#   -Dman=false          DEFAULTS TRUE, and man/meson.build:45 is
#                        `find_program('perl', required: true)` -- the manpages
#                        are generated by a bundled perl script, xmltoman. perl
#                        IS packaged here so this would work, which is exactly
#                        why it needs a decision rather than a default: kmod,
#                        eudev, elogind, ostree, linux-pam, wayland, dconf and
#                        flatpak all ship -Dman=false, and an undeclared
#                        find_program is what tools/check-recipes.sh exists to
#                        catch.
#
#   -Dgcov=false, -Dipv6=true, -Dclient=true
#                        the three that already agree with their defaults.
#                        -Dclient=true is stated because it is the option this
#                        entire package now consists of, and because
#                        `if not get_option('client')` (src/meson.build:196) is
#                        a path that expects an ALREADY-INSTALLED libpulse to
#                        link against -- setting it false here would not build a
#                        smaller package, it would fail to find itself.
#
# NOT PASSED AND NOT AN OVERSIGHT: -Dgsettings, -Dalsa, -Dbluez5, -Delogind,
# -Dopenssl, -Dudev and the rest of the daemon block. See the -Ddaemon=false
# note above -- meson never evaluates them, and passing them would imply it did.
MESON_ARGS="-Ddaemon=false -Dclient=true \
-Dglib=enabled -Ddbus=enabled \
-Dgtk=disabled -Dx11=disabled -Dsystemd=disabled -Doss-output=disabled \
-Ddoxygen=false -Dtests=false -Dman=false -Dgcov=false -Dipv6=true"
